          ],
          "content": "TECHNICAL NOTE TN-07 · Photovoltaics group"
        },
        {
          "type": "doc_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.18,
            0.095,
            0.803,
            0.119
          ],
          "level": 1,
          "content": "Silicon Solar Cells: Principles and Performance"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.244,
            0.124,
            0.74,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": "Internal technical note, sample scanned document for document parsing"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.174,
            0.199,
            0.193
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "1. History"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.094,
            0.201,
            0.894,
            0.291
          ],
          "content": "The first practical silicon solar cell was demonstrated in 1954 at Bell Labs by Daryl Chapin, Calvin Fuller and Gerald Pearson; it converted about 6% of sunlight into electricity. Early cells powered satellites such as Vanguard 1 (1958). In 1961 William Shockley and Hans-Joachim Queisser derived the detailed-balance limit for a single p-n junction, which is about 33.7% for a band gap of 1.34 eV under standard sunlight."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.304,
            0.291,
            0.322
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2. Device equations"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.336,
            0.389,
            0.354
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.1 Current-voltage characteristic"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.359,
            0.702,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": "An illuminated solar cell is modelled as a current source in parallel with a diode:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.36,
            0.384,
            0.639,
            0.416
          ],
          "content": "I = I _ {L} - I _ {0} \\Bigl [ \\exp \\left(\\frac {q V}{n k T}\\right) - 1 \\Bigr ]",
          "image_source": "images/page_0_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.422,
            0.43,
            0.439
          ],
          "content": "Setting $I = 0$ gives the open-circuit voltage:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.395,
            0.444,
            0.607,
            0.476
          ],
          "content": "V _ {o c} = \\frac {n k T}{q} \\ln \\left(\\frac {I _ {L}}{I _ {0}} + 1\\right)",
          "image_source": "images/page_0_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.49,
            0.224,
            0.508
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.2 Efficiency"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.513,
            0.64,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": "The fill factor $FF$ and the power conversion efficiency $\\eta$ are defined as:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.349,
            0.537,
            0.652,
            0.57
          ],
          "content": "F F = \\frac {P _ {m a x}}{V _ {o c} I _ {s c}}, \\quad \\eta = \\frac {F F \\cdot V _ {o c} \\cdot I _ {s c}}{P _ {i n}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_0_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.102,
            0.576,
            0.163,
            0.591
          ],
          "content": "where:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.596,
            0.734,
            0.618
          ],
          "content": "\\- $I_{\\mathrm{L}}$ — photogenerated current, approximately equal to the short-circuit current $I_{\\mathrm{sc}}$;"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.623,
            0.442,
            0.642
          ],
          "content": "\\- $I_{0}$ — dark saturation current of the diode;"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.646,
            0.641,
            0.663
          ],
          "content": "\\- $n$ — ideality factor; $k$ — Boltzmann constant; $T$ — cell temperature;"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.668,
            0.709,
            0.693
          ],
          "content": "\\- $P_{\\text{in}}$ — incident light power, 1000 W/m$^{2}$ under standard test conditions (STC)."
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.706,
            0.773,
            0.898
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.124,
                0.903,
                0.886,
                0.925
              ],
              "content": "Figure 1. Calculated $I - V$ and $P - V$ curves of a $156\\mathrm{mm}$ silicon cell ($I_{L} = 9.5\\mathrm{A}$, $I_{0} = 5\\cdot 10^{-11}\\mathrm{A}$, $n = 1$, $T = 300\\mathrm{K}$)."
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_0_chart_20.jpg"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.105,
            0.068,
            0.381,
            0.087
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3. Photovoltaic technologies"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.12,
            0.905,
            0.304
          ],
          "content": "<table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Group</td><td rowspan=\"2\">Technology</td><td colspan=\"2\">Absorber</td><td rowspan=\"2\">Typical module efficiency, %</td><td rowspan=\"2\">Market status</td></tr><tr><td>Band gap, eV</td><td>Band gap type</td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Crystalline silicon</td><td>Monocrystalline Si</td><td>1.12</td><td>indirect</td><td>20 – 23</td><td>dominant</td></tr><tr><td>Multicrystalline Si</td><td>1.12</td><td>indirect</td><td>16 – 18</td><td>declining</td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"3\">Thin film</td><td>CdTe</td><td>1.45</td><td>direct</td><td>18 – 19</td><td>utility scale</td></tr><tr><td>CIGS</td><td>1.0 – 1.7</td><td>direct</td><td>14 – 17</td><td>niche</td></tr><tr><td>Amorphous Si</td><td>1.7</td><td>amorphous</td><td>6 – 9</td><td>legacy</td></tr><tr><td>Emerging</td><td>Perovskite</td><td>1.5 – 1.6</td><td>direct</td><td>—</td><td>early commercial</td></tr></table>",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.106,
                0.098,
                0.627,
                0.115
              ],
              "content": "Table 1. Comparison of photovoltaic technologies (approximate values) ${}^{1}$"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_table_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.103,
            0.325,
            0.298,
            0.344
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4. Worked example"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.102,
            0.357,
            0.531,
            0.373
          ],
          "content": "A monocrystalline cell of  $156 \\times 156$  mm under STC has:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.38,
            0.349,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": "1 $V_{\\mathrm{oc}} = 0.67\\mathrm{V}$ and $I_{\\mathrm{sc}} = 9.5\\mathrm{A}$;"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.404,
            0.564,
            0.425
          ],
          "content": "2 fill factor $FF = 0.80$, so $P_{\\max} = 0.80 \\times 0.67 \\times 9.5 \\approx 5.1 \\mathrm{~W}$;"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.428,
            0.552,
            0.448
          ],
          "content": "3 incident power  $P_{in} = 1000 \\, W/m^{2} \\times 0.0243 \\, m^{2} \\approx 24.3 \\, W$ ;"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.454,
            0.368,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": "4 efficiency $\\eta \\approx 5.1 / 24.3\\approx 21\\%$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.101,
            0.484,
            0.248,
            0.502
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5. Conclusions"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.094,
            0.515,
            0.772,
            0.534
          ],
          "content": "• Crystalline silicon dominates the market thanks to mature manufacturing and low cost."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.093,
            0.54,
            0.742,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": "\\- Efficiency is limited by the band gap: the Shockley–Queisser limit is about 33.7%."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.092,
            0.564,
            0.702,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": "• Tandem cells, e.g. perovskite on silicon, can exceed the single-junction limit."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.102,
            0.597,
            0.691,
            0.616
          ],
          "content": "$^{1}$  Efficiency figures are typical values for commercial products and vary between manufacturers."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
